    "created_at": "2019-09-08T14:33:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-09-08T14:33:44Z",
    "body": "My argument against `security-opt` would be a fairly mild philosophical one: if the user has specifically set up sandboxing on their system, we shouldn't punch holes in it for them, in the same reason that we wouldn't engineer a VM escape in order to test things \"on bare metal.\" I admit this isn't exactly the strongest reason, but it's also not impossible to think of a scenario in which a bug in docker, combined with some unexpected security flags in a docker command, causes some data to leak on a system that was thought to be secure.\r\n\r\nThe argument against putting `:Z` in the script is a whole lot stronger: if the user accidentally runs that script from their home directory, [their entire system will become unusable](https://docs.docker.com/storage/bind-mounts/#configure-the-selinux-label). That does not seem like the kind of thing we'd like to accidentally do.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
