    "created_at": "2019-09-08T16:26:20Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-09-08T16:27:31Z",
    "body": "> I have the feeling that the integration_test might be an unnecessary abstraction. \r\n\r\nThis might be correct. I don't know if `integration_test` is necessary for our CI/CD system though.\r\n\r\n> It contains only two relevant instructions and only the second one has to be executed on a regular basis.\r\n\r\nIn principle, this is right. In practice, starship development is currently proceeding at a pretty rapid clip, and we find ourselves having to add new languages/modules into the docker image almost every release (there's a PR open right now that will involve adding Java to the image), so it's not clear how often you'll actually need to run the first command.\r\n\r\n> I would propose to get rid of integration_test\r\n\r\nHmm. I think there's value in simple, one-command testing even if it doesn't work in 1% of cases or is a little slow, because it encourages people to run the tests. In particular, telling people to 'run `integration_test`' is still a lot easier than telling them to 'read `CONTRIBUTING.md`, find the docker section, and follow the instructions there.' However, I think your next ideas are things that we should implement:\r\n\r\n> Link to docker installation instructions\r\n\r\nYes, I think this would be nice. I was barely familiar with docker when I started working on this project and it took me quite some time to get caught up.\r\n\r\n> Instruction to build image\r\n> Instruction to run test suite\r\n\r\nI think these would be good to have in a subsection.\r\n\r\n> Notes regarding SELinux\r\n\r\nYes, this is definitely a good thing to have. Perhaps a link back to the docker site's section on labels would be good here.\r\n\r\nPerhaps one way to do this would be to put notes in CONTRIBUTING.md about how to manually run the docker test, and then note that a (slow) script is available in `integration_test`",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
